+scientific_hypotheses:
+  - id: PM11-H1
+    title: Taurine Availability and Excitotoxic Calcium Stress
+    status: Unestablished
+    proposition: >-
+      Greater taurine availability at neural target sites may limit glutamate-induced calcium overload and
+      downstream cellular injury. Whether dietary or supplemental taurine produces sufficient target-site
+      exposure to influence this process in humans remains unresolved.
+    proposed_direction: Taurine availability → glutamate-induced calcium overload and excitotoxic injury
+    rationale: >-
+      The canonical taurine review describes preclinical inhibitory signalling, calcium regulation and
+      mitochondrial protection. Reduced glutamate-induced calcium influx and injury in experimental neurons
+      motivate testing this relationship within PM11.
+    finding_links:
+      supporting: []
+      contradictory_or_null: []
+      contextual:
+        - pm_id: BRS1-FM4-PM11
+          finding_id: PM11-F1
+          href: '#pm11-f1'
+          label: Excessive receptor stimulation can produce excitotoxic energy stress
+          explanation: Establishes the injury context. Taurine was not the protective intervention in this Finding.
+    evidence_sources:
+      - study_id: md_jakaria_a_taurine_nodate
+        label: Jakaria et al. (2019)
+        url: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2213231719301971
+        role: contextual
+        proposition: >-
+          Reviews experimental taurine effects on inhibitory signalling, glutamate-induced calcium influx and
+          neuronal injury.
+        limitations: >-
+          Secondary synthesis of cell and animal studies. Primary-study concentrations and exposure conditions
+          require verification.
+      - study_id: jangra_emergence_2024
+        label: Jangra et al. (2024)
+        url: https://doi.org/10.4103/1673-5374.374139
+        role: contextual
+        proposition: Reviews inhibitory, calcium-modulating and mitochondrial actions in experimental neurology.
+        limitations: >-
+          Potentially overlapping preclinical literature; review citations do not establish independent
+          replication.
+    limitations:
+      - Experimental concentrations, preparations and exposure conditions require primary-study verification.
+      - >-
+        Dietary intake, absorption and neural target-site exposure remain unresolved. Food composition
+        establishes no effective neural dose.
+      - >-
+        Preserve distinctions between receptor systems and models; reported GABA-A, GABA-B and
+        glycine-receptor actions cannot be treated as one established pathway.
+      - >-
+        The canonical page reports overall null or inconsistent human cognitive findings in Cao (2025) and
+        Moore (2026). Those endpoints do not directly test excitotoxic calcium regulation.
+      - >-
+        The adjunctive psychosis trial reported symptom findings with a null cognitive outcome. Medication
+        context and supplemental exposure limit transfer to ordinary diet and do not establish the proposed
+        mechanism.
+      - PM11-F2 concerns magnesium protection and supplies no taurine-specific support.
+      - >-
+        No dietary dose, clinical benefit, food ranking or dietary admission is established by this
+        hypothesis.
+    alternative_explanations:
+      - >-
+        Osmoregulation, mitochondrial effects and other cellular actions could contribute to experimental
+        protection.
+      - Experimental protection may depend on exposure conditions that oral intake does not reproduce.
+    required_evidence:
+      - >-
+        Verify original experimental preparations, concentrations, controls and measured calcium/injury
+        endpoints.
+      - Establish matched oral exposure and neural target engagement.
+      - Test which receptor, exchanger or mitochondrial mechanism mediates any protection.
+    provenance:
+      - >-
+        Author-approved hypothesis installation on 14 November 2026 from the rewritten canonical taurine page
+        and the reviewed PM11-H1 proposal.
+      - >-
+        Human evidence qualifications are retained from the taurine page; no new scientific appraisal or
+        dietary admission accompanies registration.
+    review_date: '2026-11-14'
+
